# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# Compression layer: Section 7 (Research Design and Methodology)

REVS = {
    "P431": ("The study works within a critical posthumanist paradigm,",
             "The study works within a critical posthumanist paradigm, and this has four consequences for the design. The first is ontological. Reality is treated as relational: entities, including technologies and households, acquire their properties through the relations in which they are enrolled. This does not mean the digester is not real; it means that what the digester is, for the purposes of the study, includes the water, the feedstock, the space, the labour and the rules that sustain it."),
    "P433": ("The second is epistemological:",
             "The second is epistemological: knowledge is situated and partial. The researcher is part of the arrangement, and the knowledge produced is knowledge from a particular position \u2014 a condition to be documented, not a weakness to be corrected by detachment. The third is methodological: if the object is a configuration, the methods must capture relations rather than variables, pointing to qualitative methods that follow actors, materials and practices, and to analytical tools that represent feedback. The fourth is axiological: the study is not neutral about justice, and it reports whether the arrangement improved or worsened the position of the people who live in it, even when the answer is unflattering."),
    "P436": ("These commitments place the study within the interpretive",
             "These commitments place the study in the interpretive and critical traditions rather than the positivist one, with implications for how quality is judged (Section 7.7). They do not imply a rejection of technical evidence: the study records gas yields, water use, cost and maintenance events, treating them as data about the configuration."),
    "P438": ("The study uses a single embedded case study,",
             "The study uses a single embedded case study, organised through the four stages of Design Research Methodology (DRM). DRM provides the internal logic of a design-oriented project \u2014 what must be clarified, described, prescribed and evaluated; the case study provides the empirical logic \u2014 how evidence is collected from a bounded instance and how claims are generalised."),
    "P439": ("Figure 5.1 shows the design.",
             "Figure 5.1 shows the design: the top band carries the two lenses that shape every stage; the four DRM stages follow with their activities; the orange line marks the iterative return from evaluation to description and prescription, the point of a design study; and the bottom band locates the sequence within the case."),
    "P444": ("Stage 1: Clarification of requirements.",
             "Stage 1: Clarification of requirements. The problem, the current situation, the desired situation and the criteria for success are established. In this study Stage 1 covers the literature review, the theoretical and conceptual frameworks, site scoping and the ethics application; its output is a set of requirements for the SETILL framework and the biogas configuration, with the criteria against which the pilot will be evaluated."),
    "P445": ("Stage 2: Descriptive study I.",
             "Stage 2: Descriptive study I. The current situation is examined in depth: key-informant interviews, resident focus groups, transect walks, household energy diaries and photovoice. The output is an evidence-based account of how energy services are currently produced in the settlement, who does the work, and what fails and why."),
    "P446": ("Stage 3: Prescriptive study.",
             "Stage 3: Prescriptive study. An intervention is developed and specified to move the situation toward the desired state: co-design workshops, the site-specific specification of the SETILL model, the specification of the biogas configuration and construction of the prototype. The output is a documented design with explicit assumptions and requirements."),
    "P447": ("Stage 4: Descriptive study II.",
             "Stage 4: Descriptive study II. The intervention is evaluated against the Stage 1 criteria and the understanding of the problem revised: pilot evaluation, justice assessment, revision of the framework and feedback of findings to the community. The stages are not a waterfall \u2014 Stage 4 feeds back to Stages 2 and 3, and a Stage 2 finding can send the researcher back to Stage 1. The design allows two full cycles and a partial third, subject to time and access."),
    "P450": ("A case study is appropriate when the research asks how or why",
             "A case study is appropriate when the research asks how or why a phenomenon works in its real-life context, when the researcher cannot manipulate behaviour, and when phenomenon and context are not clearly separable (Yin, 2009). All three conditions hold. The case is embedded rather than holistic: within the settlement there are four units of analysis \u2014 households, women's groups, the municipal service and the technology team \u2014 analysed separately and then together, because the configuration is produced at their intersection."),
    "P452": ("Flyvbjerg (2006) argues that the case study is undervalued",
             "Flyvbjerg (2006) argues that a well-chosen case can produce knowledge unavailable from statistical samples, particularly where the aim is to understand mechanisms rather than estimate frequencies \u2014 the aim here. The study does not claim its findings describe all South African informal settlements; it claims they describe a configuration of a type that recurs, and it provides enough detail about the conditions for readers to judge whether the mechanism would operate elsewhere."),
    "P455": ("The case site is an informal settlement",
             "The case site is an informal settlement in the Greater Tzaneen Local Municipality, Limpopo Province. The specific settlement will be confirmed during scoping in the first six months, using the criteria in Table 5.1, stated in advance so that the choice can be assessed."),
    "P458": ("Two further considerations shape the choice.",
             "Two further considerations shape the choice. The site must be safe for the research team, including fieldworkers who are women. And a pre-existing relationship \u2014 through the university, a partner organisation or prior research \u2014 is preferred, because entering a settlement without one is slow and can be extractive. If no site meets all seven criteria, the study will document which were relaxed and why; that documentation is part of the reflexive record."),
    "P464": ("The intended total is approximately 55 to 70 participants",
             "The intended total is approximately 55 to 70 participants across the individual interviews, plus focus group participants who may overlap. The final number will be set by information power rather than quota: Guest et al. (2020) show that thematic saturation in a homogeneous sample can occur within twelve interviews, and Hennink and Kaiser (2022) find nine to seventeen in narrower studies; this study's more heterogeneous sample and several data types imply a larger number, with saturation assessed at code level and reported."),
    "P467": ("The study uses six methods,",
             "The study uses six methods, together with observation and documentary analysis, each chosen because it produces a different kind of evidence about the configuration and each analysed in relation to the others. Table 5.3 sets out the methods, participants, research questions served and outputs."),
    "P471": ("Semi-structured interviews will be used with key informants",
             "Semi-structured interviews will be used with key informants and a subset of households. The guide is organised around the ten components but does not follow them mechanically: it begins with the person's own account of how energy is obtained and used, moves to the points at which it fails, then to the surrounding arrangements, and finally to decision-making and fairness. Interviews will be conducted in the participant's preferred language, audio-recorded with consent, transcribed and, where necessary, translated with the translation checked by a second speaker and documented as the analytical act it is."),
    "P474": ("Focus groups will be held separately for women, men, youth and tenants.",
             "Focus groups will be held separately for women, men, youth and tenants, because mixed groups in this context tend to reproduce existing authority, with older men speaking first and at length; a final mixed session will test whether the separate accounts survive when the groups are brought together. Facilitation and note-taking will be by trained fieldworkers of the same gender where possible, recorded with consent."),
    "P477": ("A transect walk is a structured walk",
             "A transect walk is a structured walk through the settlement with a resident guide, recording what is observed and what the guide says. It produces spatial evidence interviews miss: where the water points are, how far the wood is, which plots have space for a digester, where waste accumulates, which paths flood, how the settlement's edges are policed. Walks will be conducted at different times of day, and notes and photographs recorded with consent, with locations described so that individuals cannot be identified."),
    "P480": ("Diaries capture what interviews miss:",
             "Diaries capture the ordinary sequence of a day. Participating households will keep a simple diary in the local language, with pictograms for fuel types and a page for each day, supported by twice-weekly fieldworker visits because written diaries exclude participants with limited literacy. The diary records what was cooked, on what, for how long and at what cost; when the electricity was off; what was used instead; who did the work; and what went wrong. The analysis will treat it as an account produced with the researcher, not as an objective log."),
    "P483": ("Photovoice asks participants to photograph what matters to them",
             "Photovoice asks participants to photograph what matters to them and explain the images in a group discussion, shifting the framing from the researcher's questions to the participant's attention. It produces evidence about material conditions residents consider important, and a record that can be shown to the municipality. Ethical care is required: participants will be trained in consent for photography, asked not to photograph identifiable individuals without permission, and retain control over which images are used in publications."),
    "P486": ("Four workshops will be held in Stage 3.",
             "Four workshops will be held in Stage 3: the first frames the problem and tests whether the Stage 2 diagnosis is recognised; the second develops the service model \u2014 who operates the system, who pays, who decides, what happens when it fails; the third specifies the technical configuration; the fourth reviews the prototype and agrees the evaluation criteria. The workshops will use visual and physical materials rather than text, because text-based workshops exclude participants who are not confident readers, and disagreement will be recorded rather than resolved by the facilitator."),
    "P489": ("The study will also collect documents:",
             "The study will also collect documents: municipal Integrated Development Plans, waste and energy policies, records from previous interventions, equipment specifications and maintenance logs, used to establish institutional conditions and to check accounts given in interviews."),
    "P495": ("Thematic analysis is used in its reflexive form",
             "Thematic analysis is used in its reflexive form (Braun and Clarke, 2006, 2021), which treats themes as produced by the analyst's engagement with the data rather than as latent objects waiting to be found. Coding is deductive, using the ten components, and inductive, allowing codes the framework did not anticipate, through the six phases: familiarisation, initial coding, generating, reviewing, defining and naming themes, and writing. Coding will be conducted on transcripts in the original language where possible. A second coder will code ten transcripts independently; the purpose is not positivist inter-rater reliability but a check that the coding frame is applied consistently and a surfacing of the analyst's assumptions. A reflexive journal will run through the analysis, and extracts will appear in the methodology chapter to show how interpretations changed."),
    "P499": ("Situational mapping, developed within grounded theory by Clarke (2005),",
             "Situational mapping (Clarke, 2005) produces three kinds of map: situational maps of the major human and non-human elements and their relations; social worlds and arena maps of collective actors; and positional maps of discursive positions on contested issues. The method suits the study because it can represent non-human elements without reducing them to context \u2014 the digester, the water point, the meter, the landfill and the ward committee in one frame \u2014 and positional maps are particularly useful for the disagreements the study expects: on formalisation, on whether women should manage the system, on whether the municipality can be trusted."),
    "P502": ("Causal loop diagrams represent feedback:",
             "Causal loop diagrams represent feedback: how a change in one variable affects another, and how the effect returns. They will be built with participants, to represent their own accounts of why systems succeed or fail, and by the researcher, to synthesise the evidence; one for the settlement's current arrangements and one for the proposed configuration. The comparison is itself a finding, showing which loops the intervention is intended to change. The diagrams are qualitative representations, not simulation models."),
    "P506": ("Diffraction, in Barad's (2007) sense, is a practice",
             "Diffraction, in Barad's (2007) sense, reads insights through one another rather than against one another. Here it compares data sets that describe the same phenomenon from different positions: a household diary and an official's account of the same service; a woman's description of cooking and a designer's specification of the stove. The analysis asks what each account makes visible, what each obscures, and what difference the difference makes. The output is not a synthesis that resolves disagreement but an account of how the phenomenon is produced differently in different apparatuses \u2014 directly relevant to RQ3, because it shows acceptance is not a single fact about a single object."),
    "P509": ("The analysis is not only a description of the case;",
             "The analysis is also a test of the framework. For each component there are three outcomes: confirmed as important and adequately specified; confirmed as important but under-specified, and revised; or found unimportant in this case, and the framework revised to say so. The revision is documented in a table showing the original component, the evidence and the revised version \u2014 itself a contribution, because it shows what a framework looks like after it has been used."),
    "P512": ("Qualitative research is judged by criteria",
             "Qualitative research is judged by criteria that differ from those of statistical work. The study adopts the eight \u201cbig tent\u201d criteria proposed by Tracy (2010) and the trustworthiness framework of Lincoln and Guba (1985), operationalised through the strategies in Table 5.5."),
    "P515": ("Three strategies deserve emphasis.",
             "Three strategies deserve emphasis. Negative case analysis: the study will actively seek and report cases that contradict the emerging argument \u2014 a household that uses the system successfully for reasons the framework did not anticipate is as important as one that abandons it. The audit trail: all coding, sampling and analytical decisions retained and indexed, so an examiner can trace how a claim was produced. And the researcher's position: a doctoral candidate at a South African university working with a supervisor whose work informs the framework, in a settlement where the researcher is an outsider. That position shapes access and interpretation, and it will be described in the thesis rather than concealed."),
    "P519": ("The study will require ethical clearance",
             "The study will require ethical clearance from the Faculty of Engineering and the Built Environment Research Ethics Committee before any fieldwork begins. No data have been collected, and none will be collected until clearance is granted. Table 5.6 sets out the main risks and their mitigation; three issues deserve more than a line in a table."),
    "P524": ("The first is the ethics of a design study that builds something.",
             "The first is the ethics of a design study that builds something: a pilot creates expectations and relationships that outlast the research, and if the system fails after the project ends, the community bears the cost of the researcher's learning. The study manages this through C10 \u2014 the exit plan is designed before the prototype is built, and construction depends on a maintenance arrangement being agreed. The second is the ethics of representation: the settlement will appear in a thesis and possibly in policy discussions, describing poverty, failure and conflict, and draft findings will be shared with participants and their responses recorded. The third is the ethics of the framework itself: a posthumanist vocabulary risks describing people in a way that diminishes their agency, and the writing will keep human voices in the text rather than turning suffering into ontology."),
    "P528": ("Table 5.7 sets out the principal risks to the project and the responses.",
             "Table 5.7 sets out the principal risks and responses. Two matter most. Access is the condition of the whole design and cannot be assumed. Scope creep is the characteristic failure of design-oriented doctorates: the research questions are fixed, and any proposed extension will be tested against them before it is accepted."),
    "P533": ("The design has four limitations, stated in advance.",
             "The design has four limitations, stated in advance. A single case cannot support claims about prevalence; the researcher's presence changes the configuration, and the reflexive record addresses this without eliminating it; the study depends on a technology that may underperform, in which case the underperformance will be reported and treated as evidence about the configuration; and three years limits what can be said about durability, so the exit plan includes arrangements for follow-up beyond the doctoral period."),
}
